## Answers to the director's questions
1. **Story and characters without subtitles?** Yes for the shape of the story and for who is who. I got Proog the controlling guide, Emo the doubting follower, and the quarrel with Emo shouting at the machine, from picture plus voice alone. Where I was lost: (a) the key with a red gem at 6:45-6:57 and the fall; (b) what the machine does to Emo at 8:25-8:50 and whether he is alive at 9:00-9:22; (c) the first 15 s, which are a title with no voice.
2. **Voices and shouted words?** Every one of the 75 lines is now in the audio and in order, including "Hurry, Emo!" (2:10), "This is not some game." (3:03), "Emo, close your eyes. Now!" (5:41-5:45), which I could not find in cut-v2. 4:51 "Pulp!": at the cue time but the recogniser got "Paul"/"pole"; 5:44 "Now!": clear; 7:09 "Emo.": clear. The recogniser garbles the name "Proog" ("Prude", "Prug"), which suggests it may not be crisp, but it is a machine, not an ear. I can't say if the two voices sound like consistent characters; a person should listen.
3. **Picture holds or distracts?** It holds me, much more than cut-v2 did. Best: 2:50-3:00, 5:52-6:19, 7:21, 8:11-8:27. Weaknesses: figures move little in the long shots (0:30-0:50, 3:10-3:30, 4:52-5:06, 7:08-7:20 each run 12-20 s with almost nothing moving), so the dialogue scenes look posed; the first 24 s are a title and a studio card. I noticed no look change in the two characters beyond lighting (Emo's top reads teal-grey on the bridge, lighter grey in the golden halls, darker in the green room); I could not see the neckline detail at the size I looked at. I did not notice a jump cut that confused me, within the 3 s sampling.
4. **The ending 09:50-10:51?** The last image (Emo's hand, 9:03-9:22) is clear as a mood but not as an event. The credits begin at 9:27, not 9:50. Nothing is said after "it is... it is..." (8:54), so a viewer has to infer from the last frames that Emo is gone or lifeless.
5. **Playback, sync and subtitles?** No playback problems. Subtitle timing: only 6 of 75 cues could be matched at both ends within 0.5 s (the rest sit within 3 s of a neighbour, so I can only say the words are spoken around the cue). 28 of 75 cues run above 17 characters per second and 10 above 21, so fast exchanges (4:26, 6:40, 7:24, 8:00-8:20) are hard to read. I did not see the browser page render them.

## What I'd watch next time
Whether the long still shots get movement (0:30-0:50, 3:10-3:30, 7:08-7:20), whether the credits show people's names, and whether the last 30 s explains what happened to Emo.
